  "grounding_micro": "Igbo ATR: every vowel belongs to one class — +ATR: i, e, o, u; −ATR: ị, a, ọ, ụ. The dot-below is lexical, not a tone mark: o and ọ are different vowels in opposite classes. A word's ATR = the shared class of its vowels (strip tone marks first, keep dots). Mixed classes = disharmonic (compound/loanword — report, don't force a value). No vowels (syllabic nasal m̀/ń) = NotApplicable. Harmonizing affixes (e.g. participle E-, infinitive I-) have no fixed vowel: derive the ROOT's ATR first, then select the affix vowel from the pairs i↔ị, e↔a, o↔ọ, u↔ụ and copy the root's ATR — rí→èrí, gbá→àgbá; never assign an affix's ATR independently. Never guess ATR — derive it from the vowels and show the derivation.",
  "universal_atr_derivation": {
    "scope": "Applies to EVERY Igbo word — including words NOT present in this dataset. ATR is never guessed from meaning, frequency, part of speech, or resemblance to other words; it is COMPUTED from the word's vowels using the procedure below. The per-token atr_derivation fields in this dataset are worked demonstrations of this same procedure.",
    "procedure": [
      "1. Take the word exactly as written (surface form or lemma). Lowercase it.",
      "2. Remove tone diacritics ONLY: acute (U+0301), grave (U+0300), macron (U+0304). NEVER remove the dot-below (U+0323): ị, ọ, ụ are distinct -ATR vowels, not variants of i, o, u.",
      "3. List the word's vowels in order and classify each using atr_groups: i, e, o, u → Plus; ị, a, ọ, ụ → Minus.",
      "4. If every vowel is Plus → the word's ATR is Plus. If every vowel is Minus → ATR is Minus.",
      "5. If the vowels are mixed → the word is Disharmonic: a compound-domain boundary, loanword, or transcription error. Report the disharmony; do not silently assign a single ATR value.",
      "6. If the word has no vowels (syllabic nasals m̀, ḿ, ǹ, ń, ...) → ATR=NotApplicable; syllabic nasals bear tone but never participate in ATR harmony."
    ],
    "morpheme_atr_classes": {
      "statement": "Igbo morphemes divide into TWO ATR classes, and the derivation direction differs. (a) LEXICALLY FIXED morphemes (roots, nouns, most free words): vowels are inherent; compute ATR from the vowels using the procedure above. (b) ATR-DEPENDENT (harmonizing) morphemes — chiefly vocalic affixes: the vowel itself is UNDERSPECIFIED between a harmony pair and is SELECTED by the root's class. For these, ATR is never derived independently — it is copied from the controlling root. In annotations this is marked ATR_Role=Dep with ATRCtrl pointing at the controller.",
      "dependent_realization_procedure": [
        "1. Identify the controlling root in the same morphological word (for verbal prefixes: the verb root).",
        "2. Derive the ROOT's ATR from the root's own vowels (procedure above).",
        "3. Select the affix vowel from its harmony pair: +ATR root → the Plus member (i, e, o, u); −ATR root → the Minus member (ị, a, ọ, ụ).",
        "4. The affix's surface ATR = the root's ATR, by copying — not by independent computation.",
